1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,760
If you play soccer, basketball, or volleyball, there's a good chance you've heard about someone tearing their ACL — and if you're a female athlete, your risk is a lot higher than you might think.

2
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,720
Your ACL connects your thigh bone to your shin bone and keeps your knee from twisting too far.

3
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:19,600
Plant your foot, pivot hard, or land from a jump the wrong way, and it can tear.

4
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:23,360
Most people who tear it feel or hear a pop in that exact moment.

5
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:31,600
Here's the part that surprises people: female athletes are up to eight times more likely to tear an ACL than male athletes in the same sport.

6
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:41,600
At the last two Women's World Cups, twelve and a half percent of players had already had ACL reconstruction surgery before the tournament even started — nearly double the rate among the men.

7
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:44,320
Researchers point to a few reasons for this gap.

8
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:52,960
Women's ACLs tend to be smaller, and the bony notch the ligament passes through is often narrower, which leaves less room before it's stressed to the point of tearing.

9
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:58,800
Hip and knee alignment differs too, and hormonal shifts during the menstrual cycle appear to add to the risk.

10
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:05,280
Most ACL tears happen with no contact from another player at all — just a bad landing or a sudden turn.

11
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:16,640
Cutting, pivoting, jumping, and landing are the exact movements that show up constantly in soccer, basketball, and volleyball, which is exactly why these sports carry such a high rate of ACL injuries.

12
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:25,440
Two things matter most here: knowing how to respond right if an injury happens, and training that changes how you land and pivot so it's less likely to happen in the first place.

13
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:28,560
First, protect the knee right away if you suspect a tear.

14
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:32,400
Stay off it, ice it, and keep it elevated above your heart.

15
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:42,720
Don't just wait out the pain and hope it settles down — get it evaluated within days, not weeks, since delaying treatment raises the risk of additional damage to the cartilage inside the knee.

16
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:46,640
Second, train how you land and pivot before you ever get hurt.

17
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:55,280
Structured neuromuscular training programs have cut ACL injury rates by up to eighty-eight percent in young female soccer players in published studies.

18
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:07,920
The training itself isn't complicated — strengthening your quads and hamstrings gives your knee more support, and practicing landing softly with your knee tracking over your toes, instead of caving inward, takes a lot of stress off the ligament.

19
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:13,440
Build landing and strength training into your routine, and your ACL tear risk drops significantly.

20
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:22,160
And if a pop, sudden swelling, or a knee that feels like it's giving way happens after a pivot or landing, get it checked out right away instead of waiting it out.
